@misc{Laws_24306,
 abstract = {Wong and Eidsheim note that to play music is ‘to bring oneself under the influence of other bodies from the past. We perform memories, our own and those of others’. Ceci n’est pas un piano (2002), for piano, recorded text, electronics, and video, by composer and sound artist Annea Lockwood, seems to invite performative exploration of exactly this. I recently developed a new version of this piece – for performance, but subsequently a film version. This presentation explores aspects of that process.

In Ceci n’est pas un piano the pianist engages with the instrument as a site for encountering both the embodied materiality of sound and the dynamics of musical agency. Body-instrument interactions are foregrounded by the extended gestures necessary to realise certain experimental piano techniques. The musical materials weave around a recorded text: a pianist speaks of her relationship to the piano, of memories of pianos and playing, of pianos in domestic and cultural contexts, and of how one’s playing develops intersubjectively, through processes of mimicry and differentiation. This recording is relayed through the piano body, provoking sympathetic resonances. The sound-producing actions of the pianist become integrated, sonically and spatially, with the text: the speaking voice becomes a kind of piano voice.  However, the duration and character of the resonances are contingent upon the individual piano, the configuration of the technology, and the spatial acoustics: resonance is provoked by performer actions and the recording, but is not fully under control. While the text and the piece title invite us to think about pianos – what they are, where they belong – awareness of this unpredictable piano ‘voice’ transforms performance into an exploration of the dynamics of agency between subjects, ‘things’, and their contexts. Finally, the film version further explores the wider status of the instrument – its weight, both physically and metaphorically, and its materiality – in the context of this dynamic, transplanting the piano into the outside, rural environment. 
},
 address = {Leeds, UK},
 author = {Laws, Catherine},
